al cases  this week aren't there yes exactly so  there's another court case also in the  high court another judicial review  bought brought by campaigners who are  arguing that the government should stop  what is effectively financial support  for the oil and gas industry and that  support comes in the form of tax breaks  for  aiding exploration of new sites and also  decommissioning of old infrastructure  and their argument is that these  projects would be unviable without that  extra support from the government and  therefore shouldn't go ahead at all if  they're uneconomic and there's a few  other  issues for the government at the moment  related to fossil fuels they're all sort  of slightly different issues there's  also the coal mine in cumbria the  whitehaven coal mine that's the subject  of an inquiry at the moment and all of  these sorts of issues that they're  slightly different but in a sense  they're related because the government  is making big claims about being a  climate leader it's hosted cop 26 and  indeed it has done some very ambitious  things but on the other hand there are  these sort of flies in the ointment and  the campaigners are holding these up to  the government and saying what about  these victoria thanks very much indeed  and just to let you know that later in  the program we're going to be discussing  what role if any natural gas should play  as a transition fuel that's our daily  debate with economist vicki price and  the journalist donica mccarthy  so let's take a look now at some of the  day's other climate news and wildfires  produced a record amount of carbon  emissions in several regions of the  world this year according to climate  scientists from the eu's copernicus  monitoring service fires blazed across  europe siberia and north america  creating the equivalent carbon dioxide  to more than double germany's annual  emissions a copernicus scientist said  increased fire risk is due to drier and  hotter regional conditions under 