n at risk of collapse  so what are the implications of that not  just for biodiversity but also also for  people's livelihoods  well that's well that's that's right  anna so with the loss of coral reefs  there's our coastal zones depend a lot  on tourism and on fishing as well and on  trade and all of these are dependent on  coral reefs for their protection and the  products that they give the food that  they provide  and so the loss of the reefs will be a  real blow to the economies of all of the  countries in the region  so what could be done about it  well some of the re some of the threats  that we assess so overfishing can be  managed locally and need to be and  pollution is another threat we didn't we  weren't able to assess at this scale but  climate change is really the over  arching threat and that will only get  worse over the next few decades and so  that really needs the paris agreements  you know all the discussions we had at  cop26 to come to fruition and to really  uh  really take action and and and stop  carbon dioxide emissions  so some solutions at local level but do  you feel encouraged by some of the  political agreements made at cop226 but  also a cop 15 the biodiversity  conference as well  when i was at cop26 and i must say the  energy  there was tremendous both inside the  venue and outside and i think the  political agreements are always somewhat  less uh than many people would like but  that's the nature of politics but  they really show these cops really show  the commitment of governments scientists  cities of the public to really take  action the awareness is growing and so  both the the awareness that is brought  through cop26 and that that will come  through cop 15 on biodiversity we're  hoping that these will really turn the  tide on the action that is needed within  the next 10 years  to to avoid our coral reefs from  becoming from collapsing in the next few  decades  david thanks very much indeed  thank you  now every household in wales is to be  offered a free tree to plant as part of  a scheme to tackle climate change the 2  million pound scheme will allow people  to plant the trees themselves or have  one planted on their behalf by the  woodland trust more than 20 community  hubs will open across wales for  residents to collect their trees next  year  and in the rush to plant millions of  trees to help develop biodiversity and  mitigate against the effects of climate  change hundreds of thousands of plastic  tree guards are used every year now  they're necessary to protect new trees  as they grow but they're often forgotten  about after use and end up as litter  well let's hear now from mike appleton  from the yorkshire dales millennium  trust who's working to change that  [Music]  so this year we're aiming to remove 40  000  guards from the from the landscape  working with partners like like tubex to  make sure that they're recycled  correctly  i think for us it's important that it's  uh  to note that there's a community effort  to renew guards  we will bring a group out from kind of  an urban area of leeds or blackburn or  bradford and actually come out and spend  the day spend the day in the dales you  know asylum seekers and refugees who you  have to kind of get away from the  stresses that are alive  we've got one that's uh that sheep's  wool which uh you know everybody  especially in the girls big farming  getting quite excited about we've got a  couple of  classes we've got traditional um  cardboard tubes as well in various  heights  compostable  plastics as well which need to be  composted at 50 degrees centigrade  [Music]  do stay tuned coming up after the break  i'll be joined by the economist vicky  price and the climate columnist for the  independent donaka mccarthy and we're  going to be talking transition fuels and  whether we can look forward to flying  off on our holidays on hydrogen-powered  planes  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news and we're  getting straight to discussing the  climate issues of the day now with  economist vicki price and climate  columnist for the independent donor  mccarthy so very good evening to both of  you nice to see you both  so first of all the uk government has  come under fire for promising to provide  more than a billion dollars of funding  to a liquefied natural gas project in  mozambique a group of climate scientists  say providing that funding is  incompatible with climate targets and  that undermines the uk's global  leadership on climate change but the  government argues the gas has a vital  role to play in a clean energy  transition so vicky first of all what do  you think about the uk  continuing to support  projects like this abroad we'll talk  abroad first before we talk domestically  do you think it's a good idea  well this particular one of course if  you think about the liquefied natural  gas it's quite useful has been very  useful in the uk recently has been  useful in europe and it will be quite  useful in asia where it's meant to be  going from mozambique because of course  when you look at what's happening with  coal fire power stations and coal  um produce considerably more and in fact  produce something like 45 more than uh  gas so gas as a transition  um product if you like is in fact very  useful particularly for developing  countries where the investment that  would need to be made otherwise is  beyond their means  right now okay so what do you make  you're well you're shaking your head  donnica do you not agree with that then  well to be investing billions globally  in new fossil fuels when we've got nine  years to get to net zero is is insanity  the recent research in the in the  journal nature said we need to leave 60  60 of existing gas in the ground if  we're going to have just a 50  chance of not breaching 1.5 degrees  centigrade so the britain should be  doing is investing in enabling  developing countries go to directly to  renewables rather than making them  dependent on new fossil fuel investment  and we have to remember the investment  in mozambique it's a tiny fraction of  what britain's investing globally it's  just not 0.1  over the next 10 years british banks  government and oil companies are  proposing to invest  750 billion in new fossil fuels  including glass it's untenable  what about domestically then vicky i  know that shell talked last week about  the the need for continued investment in  oil and gas they say it's critical to  the country's energy security  what about the uk  i think up to a point that's true uh but  of course what needs to happen is it  should go hand in hand with carbon  capture and storage and i think that's  the development that we need to see  happening i know it's been talked about  for quite some time so far it hasn't  been developed to scale but i think it  can and the right investment will make  quite a big difference there so for the  time being i think the transition period  is such that if we don't have enough  security of supply here we will see the  the types of increases in prices we've  seen very recently and which of course  will turn the consumers you know off the  whole idea of climate change if it's not  done properly so yes i think some  balance uh in the development is  absolutely essential but i don't  disagree that we need to get to the  right end uh you know when we look at  what is required in terms of climate  change measures both mitigation and also  adaptation and donor the uk government  says there's likely to be an ongoing but  diminishing demand for oil and gas that  the uk couldn't commit to phasing out  the energy sources entirely for now do  you think that's a fair position  um  nobody's talking about phasing out um  oil and gas immediately what we're  talking about is a transition over maybe  a decade what we are talking about is  stop investing in new fossil fuels we  have more than enough gas to to burn the  planet with more than enough oil so  investing  five trillion dollars over the next 10  years in new fossil fuels which the  government is supporting through the  through our banks etc it doesn't make  any sense so that's the situation we  have to do is stop the new investments  in fossil fuels and actually invest  the 5 trillion in renewables for both uk  and foreign developing countries okay  let's move on to our next subject shall  we because earlier in the show we heard  how hydrogen-powered planes  could make flying more sustainable now  hydrogen doesn't produce any co2  emissions its only waste product is  water so could this be the start of  guilt-free flying i mean vicki  everyone's going to like the sound of  that so what do you make of this project  to try to develop a liquid hydrogen fuel  aircraft  well if it were to work that would be  absolutely brilliant uh it's been talked  about for some time but the interesting  thing is that the airline sector that  accounts for something like two percent  of global co2 emissions are present but  rising has already managed to improve  its efficiency very significantly but i  think that would make a huge difference  i mean we know that it isn't going to  solve the problem completely in terms of  aviation because there are lots of other  things that come out in the atmosphere  when you fly at high altitudes but it  will certainly make a big big difference  because basically the co2 would no  longer be emitted it would just the  waste would be water but if you're able  to do long distance and have the right  aircraft that is able to support it then  obviously will make a tremendous  difference in terms of how we can  envisage the future in a clean sort of  net zero environment which hydrogen i  think can do and there are expectations  that it might actually by 2050 supply  something like 25 to 35 percent of all  energy needs  delica how optimistic are you  i think there is an extraordinary press  release by the government if you read  the detail in i