king them  dependent on new fossil fuel investment  and we have to remember the investment  in mozambique it's a tiny fraction of  what britain's investing globally it's  just not 0.1  over the next 10 years british banks  government and oil companies are  proposing to invest  750 billion in new fossil fuels  including glass it's untenable  what about domestically then vicky i  know that shell talked last week about  the the need for continued investment in  oil and gas they say it's critical to  the country's energy security  what about the uk  i think up to a point that's true uh but  of course what needs to happen is it  should go hand in hand with carbon  capture and storage and i think that's  the development that we need to see  happening i know it's been talked about  for quite some time so far it hasn't  been developed to scale but i think it  can and the right investment will make  quite a big difference there so for the  time being i think the transition period  is such that if we don't have enough  security of supply here we will see the  the types of increases in prices we've  seen very recently and which of course  will turn the consumers you know off the  whole idea of climate change if it's not  done properly so yes i think some  balance uh in the development is  absolutely essential but i don't  disagree that we need to get to the  right end uh you know when we look at  what is required in terms of climate  change measures both mitigation and also  adaptation and donor the uk government  says there's likely to be an ongoing but  diminishing demand for oil and gas that  the uk couldn't commit to phasing out  the energy sources entirely for now do  you think that's a fair position  um  nobody's talking about phasing out um  oil and gas immediately what we're  talking about is a transition over maybe  a decade what we are talking about is  stop investing in new fossil fuels we  have more than enough gas to to burn the  planet with more than enough oil so  investing  five trillion dollars over the next 10  years in new fossil fuels which the  government is supporting through the  through our banks etc it doesn't make  any sense so that's the situation we  have to do is stop the new investments  in fossil fuels and actually invest  the 5 trillion in renewables for both uk  and foreign developing countries okay  let's move on to our next subject shall  we because earlier in the show we heard  how hydrogen-powered planes  could make flying more sustainable now  hydrogen doesn't produce any co2  emissions its only waste product is  water so could this be the start of  guilt-free flying i mean vicki  everyone's going to like the sound of  that so what do you make of this project  to try to develop a liquid hydrogen fuel  aircraft  well if it were to work that would be  absolutely brilliant uh it's been talked  about for some time but the interesting  thing is that the airline sector that  accounts for something like two percent  of global co2 emissions are present but  rising has already managed to improve  its efficiency very significantly but i  think that would make a huge difference  i mean we know that it isn't going to  solve the problem completely in terms of  aviation because there are lots of other  things that come out in the atmosphere  when you fly at high altitudes but it  will certainly make a big big difference  because basically the co2 would no  longer be emitted it would just the  waste would be water but if you're able  to do long distance and have the right  aircraft that is able to support it then  obviously will make a tremendous  difference in terms of how we can  envisage the future in a clean sort of  net zero environment which hydrogen i  think can do and there are expectations  that it might actually by 2050 supply  something like 25 to 35 percent of all  energy needs  delica how optimistic are you  i think there is an extraordinary press  release by the government if you read  the detail in i